 "stop": "20 lines",
 "poem": "Cricket-filled, where it pitched itself\nOn the prairie boughs of the forest,\nAnd where the fireside chimney\nMade its white effulgence appear,\nThough the roof was not all opaque and\nBlack with smoke as far below our\nEars grew fainter still; until\nThe moon in her midnight glow\nCame down upon us like a shadow,\nScattering it on the forest;\nAnd we were silent save for our\nSounds and shadows; until one morning,\nWhen the sky looked clear above\nEarth's high ridge, we heard again\nA cricket crackle; then again,\nWhen the moon had gone out again\nRusset-browed, and rosy-golden eyes,\nWe heard again a cricket\nClack! clack! clack, by way of welcome, and\nPity that we were not there again.",
